In two dimension, the position vectors $\mathbf{r}_{1}$ and $\mathbf{r}_{2}$ represented as

\begin{array}{l}
\mathbf{r}_{1}=v t \hat{\mathbf{i}}-\frac{1}{2} g t^{2} \mathbf{j} ...(i) \\
\mathbf{r}_{2}=v t(-\hat{\mathbf{i}})-\frac{1}{2} g t^{2}(\mathbf{j})...(ii)
\end{array}

$\because$ We know that, when the two vectors are mutually perpendicular, i.e.

\theta=90^{\circ}

So,

\begin{array}{l}
\mathbf{r}_{1} \cdot \mathbf{r}_{2}=r_{1} r_{2} \cos 90^{\circ} \\
\mathbf{r}_{1} \cdot \mathbf{r}_{2}=0
\end{array}

Substituting the values $\mathbf{r}_{1}$ and $\mathbf{r}_{2}$ in the above relation, we get

\left((v t) \hat{\mathbf{i}}-\frac{1}{2} g t^{2} \mathbf{j}\right) \cdot\left(v t(-\hat{\mathbf{i}})-\frac{1}{2} g t^{2}(\mathbf{j})\right)=0

$-v^{2} t^{2}+\frac{1}{4} 4 g^{2} t^{4}=0($ where, $\hat{\mathbf{i}} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{i}}=\hat{\mathbf{j}} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{j}}=\mathbf{k} \cdot \mathbf{k}=1)$
\begin{aligned}
v^{2} t^{2} &=\frac{1}{4} g^{2} t^{4} \\
\Rightarrow \quad v^{2} &=\frac{1}{4} g^{2} t^{2}
\end{aligned}

$\therefore$ Magnitude of velocity of the particles, $v=\frac{1}{2} g t$
We know that, separation distance between particles at a time $t$
\begin{array}{c}
\Delta x=2 v t \\
\Delta x=2 \times v \times \frac{2 v}{g} \Rightarrow \dot{\Delta} x=\frac{4 v^{2}}{g}
\end{array}

